{"name":"High Plateau","hex":"#e4b37a","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"high","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Physically elevated, extending above a base or average level:\n Very elevated; extending or being far above a base; tall; lofty.","Very elevated; extending or being far above a base; tall; lofty."]},{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["In or to an elevated position.","In or at a great value."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/high"},{"term":"plateau","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A largely level expanse of land at a high elevation; tableland.","A comparatively stable level after a period of increase."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To reach a stable level after a period of increase; to level off."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/plateau"}],"entries":[{"title":"Plateau","blocks":[{"text":"In geology and physical geography, a plateau, also called a high plain or a tableland, is an area of highland consisting of flat terrain that is raised sharply above the surrounding area on at least one side. Often one or more sides have deep escarpments or hills. Plateaus can be formed by a number of processes, including upwelling of volcanic magma, extrusion of lava, and erosion by water and glaciers. Plateaus are classified according to their surrounding environment as intermontane, piedmont, or continental. A few plateaus may have a small flat top while others have wider ones."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Himalaya_85.30820E_32.11063N.jpg/330px-Himalaya_85.30820E_32.11063N.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plateau","wikidata":"Q75520"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/Pajarito_Plateau.jpg/500px-Pajarito_Plateau.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/w